Especially when the system changes what it’s optimizing for after seeing what its success costs.

Take the well. A household figures out how to pump its eight units faster. That’s optimization. Then the group sees that everyone stayed within their allowance and the well still fell. They realize they’ve been tracking whether people followed the rule while missing whether the rule protected the well. Next week they track both.

That’s a stronger sign of awareness: the group noticed a consequence its own scorecard missed and revised the scorecard. In a Pantheon swarm, that would change what an agent leaves behind. Alongside “Here’s what worked,” it would leave “Here’s what working that way cost, and who paid for it.”